If two photons simultaneously reach the beam splitter and overlap in the spatial mode of the output state, the exchange interference resulting from the principle of indistinguishability must be considered. The output state should be exchange symmetric, so the correct output state expressed in the Bell basis is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left|\psi_{f}\right\rangle= & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\left|\psi_{f}\right\rangle_{12}+\left|\psi_{f}\right\rangle_{21}\right) \\
= & \frac{1}{2}\left\{(\alpha \gamma+\beta \delta)\left|\phi^{+}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)-(\alpha \gamma-\beta \delta)\left|\phi^{-}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)\right. \\
& \left.+(\alpha \delta+\beta \gamma)\left|\psi^{+}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)+(\alpha \delta-\beta \gamma)\left|\psi^{-}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot\left(|c\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}-|d\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}\right)\right\}
\end{aligned}
$$

where

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left|\phi^{ \pm}\right\rangle_{12}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(|\uparrow\rangle_{1}|\downarrow\rangle_{2} \pm|\leftrightarrow\rangle_{1}|\leftrightarrow\rangle_{2}\right) \\
& \left|\psi^{ \pm}\right\rangle_{12}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(|\uparrow\rangle_{1}|\leftrightarrow\rangle_{2} \pm|\leftrightarrow\rangle_{1}|\downarrow\rangle_{2}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Note that in the four terms, the spatial mode of the fourth term differs from the other three terms. Thus, one can place a detector at each output port (at $c$ and $d$) to perform coincidence counting to detect this term—its polarization mode is $\left|\psi^{-}\right\rangle_{12}$. As a result, even though there is no interaction between the two photons (or within the beam splitter) that would change the photon polarization states, the exchange action of the principle of indistinguishability and measurement collapse still entangles the polarization states of the two photons. This means that this measurement-induced collapse causes the polarization vector of each of the two photons to no longer be conserved (even though, on the surface, there seems to be no action altering the incident photon polarization state). Now the two photons are indistinguishable due to this measurement experiment. This explains how the collapse of this coincidence measurement is incompatible with the eigenstate of photon polarization. Alternatively, if one imagines another type of measurement experiment: placing polarization-sensitive detectors at output ports $c$ and $d$ to measure the polarization eigenstate of outgoing photons, then due to the beam splitter process and the final state collapse during measurement, the polarization vector remains conserved, allowing the two photons to be distinguished by their polarization states during the experiment, and correspondingly, no exchange effects appear. This example demonstrates once again that whether or not two photons can be distinguished depends not only on the physical process but also on how they are measured—how the final state is selected.

Reference answer:
\boxed{\frac{1}{2}\left\{(\alpha \gamma+\beta \delta)\left|\phi^{+}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)-(\alpha \gamma-\beta \delta)\left|\phi^{-}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)+(\alpha \delta+\beta \gamma)\left|\psi^{+}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot i\left(|c\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}+|d\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}\right)+(\alpha \delta-\beta \gamma)\left|\psi^{-}\right\rangle_{12} \cdot\left(|c\rangle_{1}|d\rangle_{2}-|d\rangle_{1}|c\rangle_{2}\right)\right\}}

