For spin \(s_z=\pm \tfrac12\), the single-particle energies are
\[
\varepsilon_{\mathbf p,\pm}
=\frac{p^2}{2m}\pm\mu_{\mathrm B}H.
\]
At \(T=0\), all states satisfying \(\varepsilon_{\mathbf p,\pm}\leq\mu(0)\) are occupied. Hence
\[
\frac NV
=\frac{(2m)^{3/2}}{6\pi^2\hbar^3}
\left[
\bigl(\mu(0)+\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr)_{+}^{3/2}
+\bigl(\mu(0)-\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr)_{+}^{3/2}
\right],
\]
where \(x_+=\max(x,0)\).

Define the zero-field Fermi energy
\[
\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}
=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\left(3\pi^2\frac NV\right)^{2/3}.
\]
Therefore, while both spin populations are occupied,
\[
\bigl[\mu(0)+\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr]^{3/2}
+\bigl[\mu(0)-\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr]^{3/2}
=2\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}^{3/2}.
\]
For a weak field, this gives
\[
\mu(0)=\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}
-\frac{(\mu_{\mathrm B}H)^2}{4\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}}
+O(H^4).
\]
If \(\mu_{\mathrm B}H\geq 2^{-1/3}\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}\), the gas is fully spin-polarized and
\[
\mu(0)=2^{2/3}\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}-\mu_{\mathrm B}H.
\]

So the final answer is
\[
\boxed{
\begin{cases}
\bigl[\mu(0)+\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr]^{3/2}
+\bigl[\mu(0)-\mu_{\mathrm B}H\bigr]^{3/2}
=2\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}^{3/2},
& \mu_{\mathrm B}H<2^{-1/3}\varepsilon_{\mathrm F},\\[2mm]
\mu(0)=2^{2/3}\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}-\mu_{\mathrm B}H,
& \mu_{\mathrm B}H\geq2^{-1/3}\varepsilon_{\mathrm F},
\end{cases}
\quad
\varepsilon_{\mathrm F}
=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\left(\frac{3\pi^2N}{V}\right)^{2/3}
}
\;(\mathrm{J}).
\]

