According to the problem, the electromagnetic wave is a plane monochromatic wave, which can be expressed as:

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{E}=\boldsymbol{E}_{0} e^{-\mathrm{i} \omega t} \tag{5}
\end{equation*}

Equation (4) can then be written as:

\begin{equation*}
\ddot{\boldsymbol{r}}+\omega_{0}^{2} \boldsymbol{r}+2 \delta \dot{\boldsymbol{r}}=\frac{e}{m} \boldsymbol{E}_{0} e^{-\mathrm{i} \omega t} \tag{6}
\end{equation*}

where $\omega_{0}=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}$ is the "natural" frequency of electron oscillation within an atom (or molecule), $\delta=g / 2m$ is the damping coefficient, and $\boldsymbol{E}_{0}$ is the amplitude of the electric field in the electromagnetic wave.

We assume that the solution to equation (6) has the following form:

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{r}=\boldsymbol{r}_{0} e^{-\mathrm{i} \omega t} \tag{7}
\end{equation*}

where $\boldsymbol{r}_{0}$ is the amplitude of the electron displacement. Substituting equation (7) into equation (6) and performing the necessary differentiation, we obtain:

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{r}_{0}=\frac{(e / m) \boldsymbol{E}_{0}}{\left(\omega_{0}^{2}-\omega^{2} - 2\mathrm{i} \delta \omega\right)} \tag{8}
\end{equation*}

Therefore, under the influence of an external electric field of the plane wave, the non-polar molecule acquires a dipole moment:

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{p}=e \boldsymbol{r}=\frac{e^{2} \boldsymbol{E}}{m\left(\omega_{0}^{2}-\omega^{2} - 2\mathrm{i} \delta \omega\right)} \tag{9}
\end{equation*}

By definition, the expression for the polarization vector of the medium is:

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{P}=N \boldsymbol{p}=\frac{e^{2} N \boldsymbol{E}}{m\left(\omega_{0}^{2}-\omega^{2} - 2\mathrm{i} \delta \omega\right)} \tag{10}
\end{equation*}

where $N$ is the number of molecules per unit volume. From equation (10), it can be seen that the polarization vector of the medium is proportional to the external electric field, and when $\delta \neq 0$, the proportionality coefficient is a complex number.

\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{P}=\varepsilon_{0} \alpha \boldsymbol{E} \tag{11}
\end{equation*}

The coefficient $\alpha$ is called the polarizability of the medium in this context. By comparing equations (10) and (11), we derive:

\begin{equation*}
\alpha=\frac{e^{2} N}{\varepsilon_{0} m\left(\omega_{0}^{2}-\omega^{2} - 2\mathrm{i} \delta \omega\right)} \tag{12}
\end{equation*}

Reference answer:
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{e^{2} N}{\varepsilon_{0} m\left(\omega_{0}^{2}-\omega^{2} - 2\mathrm{i} \delta \omega\right)}}

First, calculate the average interaction energy \(u\) between an atom and its six nearest neighbor atoms. Given the assumption that metal atoms \(A\) and \(B\) are randomly and uniformly distributed over all positions, on average, each lattice site in the binary alloy has \(A\) atoms and \(B\) atoms as nearest neighbors in the ratios \(\frac{N_{A}}{N}\) and \(\frac{N_{B}}{N}\) respectively. Likewise, the probability for the occurrence of atoms \(A\) and \(B\) themselves at any given site is \(\frac{N_{A}}{N}\) and \(\frac{N_{B}}{N}\) respectively. Therefore, the average interaction energy between an atom and its nearest neighbors is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
u & =6\left[(-J) \frac{N_{A}}{N}+J \frac{N_{B}}{N}\right] \frac{N_{A}}{N}+6\left[(-J) \frac{N_{B}}{N}+J \frac{N_{A}}{N}\right] \frac{N_{B}}{N} \\
& =6\left[(-J) \frac{N_{A}^{2}+N_{B}^{2}}{N^{2}}+J \frac{2 N_{A} N_{B}}{N^{2}}\right]=-\frac{6 J}{N^{2}}\left(N_{A}-N_{B}\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the total interaction energy of the binary alloy composed of \(N_{A}\) metal atoms \(A\) and \(N_{B}\) metal atoms \(B\) is:
\[
U=\frac{N}{2} u=-\frac{3 J}{N}\left(N_{A}-N_{B}\right)^{2}=-3 N J x^{2}
\]

Reference answer:
\boxed{-3 N J x^{2}}

For any state vector $|\psi\rangle$, in the $x$ representation

\begin{equation*}
\psi^{\prime}(x)=\hat{A} \psi(x)=\int A\left(x, x^{\prime}\right) \psi\left(x^{\prime}\right) \mathrm{d} x^{\prime} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

In the $p$ representation

\begin{equation*}
\phi^{\prime}(p)=\hat{A} \phi(p)=\int A\left(p, p^{\prime}\right) \phi\left(p^{\prime}\right) \mathrm{d} p^{\prime} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

Note that

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x)=\left(\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar}\right)^{1 / 2} \int \phi(p) \mathrm{e}^{\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} p x} \mathrm{d} p \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

Substitute equation (3) into equation (1), and multiply both sides by $\left(\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar}\right)^{1 / 2} \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} p x}$ on the left, integrating over $x$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar}\right) \int \psi^{\prime}(x) \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} p x} \mathrm{d} x &= \phi^{\prime}(p) \\
& = \int \mathrm{d} x \left(\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar}\right)^{1/2} \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} p x} \int \mathrm{d} x^{\prime} A\left(x, x^{\prime}\right) \int \mathrm{d} p^{\prime} \phi\left(p^{\prime}\right) \mathrm{e}^{\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} x^{\prime} p^{\prime}}\left(\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar}\right)^{1/2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Comparing with equation (2), it follows that

$$
A\left(p, p^{\prime}\right)=\frac{1}{2 \pi \hbar} \iint A\left(x, x^{\prime}\right) \mathrm{e}^{\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\left(p^{\prime} x^{\prime}-p x\right)} \mathrm{d} x \mathrm{d} x^{\prime}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{A\left(p, p^{\prime}\right)=\frac{1}{2 \pi} \iint A\left(x, x^{\prime}\right) \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\left(p^{\prime} x^{\prime}-p x\right)} \mathrm{d} x \mathrm{d} x^{\prime}}

