\begin{equation*}
\hat{T}(a) \psi_{k}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{-a \frac{d}{d x}} \psi_{k}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k a} \psi_{k}(x) \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{e}^{-a \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}} \psi_{k}(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-a)^{n}}{n!} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d}^{n}}{\mathrm{d} x^{n}} \psi_{k}(x) = \psi_{k}(x-a) \tag{4}
\end{equation*}

Now introduce the function $u_{k}(x)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{k}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{-i k x} \psi_{k}(x), \, \text{that is}, \, \psi_{k}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k x} u_{k}(x) \tag{5}
\end{equation*}

Combining Equations (3), (4), and (5), we have

$$
\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k(x-a)} u_{k}(x-a) = \psi_{k}(x-a) = \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k a} \psi_{k}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k a} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k x} u_{k}(x)
$$

From this, we find that the function $u_{k}(x)$ satisfies the condition

\begin{equation*}
u_{k}(x) = u_{k}(x-a) \tag{6}
\end{equation*}

Equation (6) indicates that $u_{k}(x)$ is a periodic function of $x$ with a period of $a$. Therefore, the eigenfunctions of the operator $\hat{T}(a)$ are

$$
\psi_{k}(x) = u_{k}(x) \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k x}
$$

This is an amplitude-modulated plane wave.

Reference answer:
\boxed{\psi_{k}(x) = u_{k}(x) \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k x}}

