Note: You cannot use the \(v\) calculated earlier and apply \(a = \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t}\) to find \(a\). The \(\frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t}\) calculated this way is not the acceleration at point \((1,0,0)\) in space at time \(t\), but rather the rate of change of velocity of a point on the rigid body.

\[
\frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t} = \lim_{\Delta t \rightarrow 0} \frac{v(t + \Delta t) - v(t)}{\Delta t}
\]

The points at \(t\) and \(t+\Delta t\) are different points on the rigid body, so this rate of change of velocity is not the acceleration of a point at \((1,0,0)\) in space.

