& a = \frac{m_{2} g}{m_{1} + m_{2}}, \quad T = \frac{m_{1} m_{2} g}{m_{1} + m_{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $m_{2}$ has no motion in the horizontal direction, there is no interaction force between $M$ and $m_{2}$. Considering $M$ and the pulley as a system, the horizontal forces include a rightward force $F$ and a leftward tension $T$ in the rope. To keep $M$ stationary:

$$
F = T = \frac{m_{1} m_{2} g}{m_{1} + m_{2}}
$$

Applying Newton's second law in the vertical direction for $m_{1}$, $m_{2}$, and $M$ (including the pulley) and summing the equations gives:

$$
\begin{gathered}
m_{2} a = \left(M + m_{1} + m_{2}\right) g - N \\
N = \left(M + m_{1} + m_{2}\right) g - \frac{m_{2}^{2} g}{m_{1} + m_{2}} \\
= \left(M + m_{1} + \frac{m_{1} m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}}\right) g
\end{gathered}
$$

Note: $m_{2}$ is subjected to an upward tension from the rope, while the pulley experiences a downward tension $T$ from the rope.

Reference answer:
\boxed{\frac{m_{1} m_{2} g}{m_{1} + m_{2}}, \left(M + m_{1} + \frac{m_{1} m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}}\right) g}

