Let \(\{\hat{\mathbf x},\hat{\mathbf y},\hat{\mathbf z}\}\) denote the rotating coordinate basis. The particle’s position is

\[
\mathbf r(t)=f(t)\hat{\mathbf x}+g(t)\hat{\mathbf y}.
\]

The rotation axis lies in the \(xz\) plane and makes \(45^\circ\) with both axes, so

\[
\boldsymbol{\omega}
=\omega\cos45^\circ\,\hat{\mathbf x}
+\omega\cos45^\circ\,\hat{\mathbf z}
=\frac{\omega}{\sqrt2}
\left(\hat{\mathbf x}+\hat{\mathbf z}\right).
\]

The velocity measured in the stationary frame is related to that measured in the rotating frame by

\[
\mathbf v_{\mathrm{stationary}}
=
\left(\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt}\right)_{\mathrm{rotating}}
+\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf r.
\]

The relative velocity is

\[
\left(\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt}\right)_{\mathrm{rotating}}
=f'(t)\hat{\mathbf x}+g'(t)\hat{\mathbf y}.
\]

Meanwhile,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf r
&=\frac{\omega}{\sqrt2}
(\hat{\mathbf x}+\hat{\mathbf z})
\times
\left(f(t)\hat{\mathbf x}+g(t)\hat{\mathbf y}\right)\\
&=\frac{\omega}{\sqrt2}
\left[-g(t)\hat{\mathbf x}
+f(t)\hat{\mathbf y}
+g(t)\hat{\mathbf z}\right].
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore,

\[
\mathbf v_{\mathrm{stationary}}
=
\left[f'(t)-\frac{\omega g(t)}{\sqrt2}\right]\hat{\mathbf x}
+
\left[g'(t)+\frac{\omega f(t)}{\sqrt2}\right]\hat{\mathbf y}
+
\frac{\omega g(t)}{\sqrt2}\hat{\mathbf z}.
\]

So the final answer is \(\boxed{\left[f'(t)-\frac{\omega g(t)}{\sqrt2}\right]\hat{\mathbf x}+\left[g'(t)+\frac{\omega f(t)}{\sqrt2}\right]\hat{\mathbf y}+\frac{\omega g(t)}{\sqrt2}\hat{\mathbf z}}\)(unit of velocity).

