problem_statement:
A three-dimensional rotor system with quantized energy (having two degrees of rotational freedom and no translational motion) follows Boltzmann statistics. Calculate the heat capacity of each rotor at high temperatures. The energy of the rotor is given by

$$
\varepsilon_{j}=j(j+1) \hbar^{2} / 2 I=A j(j+1)
$$

where $I$ is the moment of inertia of the rotor, the rotational quantum number $j=0,1,2, \cdots$, Planck's constant $\hbar=1.055 \times 10^{-34} \mathrm{~J} \cdot \mathrm{~s}$, and $A=\hbar^{2} / 2 I$. The degeneracy of the energy level $\varepsilon_{j}$ is $g_{j}=2 j+1$. The partition function of the rotor is

\begin{align*}
Z & =\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}(2 j+1) \exp \left[-\frac{A j(j+1)}{k T}\right] \\
& =2 \exp \left(\frac{A}{4 k T}\right) \sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\left(j+\frac{1}{2}\right) \exp \left[-\frac{A}{k T}\left(j+\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}\right]
\end{align*}

Using the Euler formula, the expression can be obtained as

\begin{equation*}
Z=\left(\frac{k T}{A}+\frac{1}{12}\right) \exp \left(\frac{A}{4 k T}\right)
\end{equation*}

reference_solution:
The heat capacity of the rotor is

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}=\frac{k}{1+A / 12 k T}-\frac{A}{12 T(1+A / 12 k T)^{2}}
