Let the velocities of the two particles after the collision be $v_{1 \mathrm{c}}, v_{2 \mathrm{c}}$. The total momentum is zero,

$$
m_{1} \boldsymbol{v}_{1 \mathrm{c}} + m_{2} \boldsymbol{v}_{2 \mathrm{c}} = 0
$$

$\boldsymbol{v}_{1 \mathrm{c}}$ and $\boldsymbol{v}_{2 \mathrm{c}}$ are in opposite directions, and their magnitudes have the following relation:

\begin{equation*}
v_{2 \mathrm{c}} = \frac{m_{1}}{m_{2}} v_{1 \mathrm{c}} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

Due to the elastic collision, mechanical energy is conserved,

\begin{align*}
& \frac{1}{2} m_{1} v_{1 \mathrm{c}}^{2} + \frac{1}{2} m_{2} v_{2 \mathrm{c}}^{2} = \frac{1}{2} m_{1} v_{10 \mathrm{c}}^{2} + \frac{1}{2} m_{2} v_{20 \mathrm{c}}^{2} \\
& \quad = \frac{1}{2} m_{1}\left(\frac{m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}\right)^{2} + \frac{1}{2} m_{2}\left(\frac{m_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}\right)^{2} \tag{2} \\
& \quad = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{m_{1} m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}^{2}
\end{align*}

Solving equations (1) and (2),

$$
v_{1 \mathrm{c}} = \frac{m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}, \quad v_{2 \mathrm{c}} = \frac{m_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{v_{1 \mathrm{c}} = \frac{m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}, v_{2 \mathrm{c}} = \frac{m_{1}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} v_{10}}

