The particles of a Fermi gas have three non-degenerate energy levels $0, \varepsilon, 2 \varepsilon$. The gas can have at most 2 particles. Let the occupation numbers for the three single-particle energy levels be $N_{0}, N_{1}, N_{2}$ respectively. Then, the number of particles in the gas, $N$, is given by

\[
N = N_{0} + N_{1} + N_{2} \tag{1}
\]

The energy of the gas, $E$, is given by

\[
E = \left(N_{1} + 2N_{2}\right) \varepsilon \tag{2}
\]

Thus, the grand partition function of the Fermi gas is

\[
\begin{align*}
Z = & \sum_{N=0}^{2} \lambda^{N} \sum_{\left\{N_{1}, N_{2}\right\}} \exp \left(-\frac{E}{kT}\right) \\
= & 1 + \lambda[1 + \exp (-\varepsilon / kT) + \exp (-2\varepsilon / kT)] \\
& + \lambda^{2} \exp (-\varepsilon / kT)[1 + \exp (-\varepsilon / kT) + \exp (-2\varepsilon / kT)] \tag{3}
\end{align*}
\]

where $\lambda = \exp (\mu / kT) = \exp (-\alpha)$ is the fugacity of the gas, $\mu$ is the chemical potential of the gas, and $k$ is the Boltzmann constant.

Reference answer:
\boxed{1 + \lambda[1 + \exp (-\varepsilon / kT) + \exp (-2\varepsilon / kT)] + \lambda^{2} \exp (-\varepsilon / kT)[1 + \exp (-\varepsilon / kT) + \exp (-2\varepsilon / kT)]}

