problem_statement:
Consider the mixing states of two types of neutrinos, $
u_1$ and $
u_2$, corresponding to the electron and muon Dirac neutrinos, with masses $m_1$ and $m_2$, respectively:
$$
 egin{gathered}

u_{\mathrm{e}}=
u_{1} \cos 	heta+
u_{2} \sin 	heta \

u_{\mu}=-
u_{1} \sin 	heta+
u_{2} \cos 	heta
\end{gathered}
$$
where $	heta$ is called the mixing angle. The mass term of their Hamiltonian is given by
$$
H=m_1  ar{
u}_{1} 
u_{1} + m_2  ar{
u}_{2} 
u_{2}
$$
Express the Hamiltonian matrix elements in the $
u_e$ and $
u_\mu$ representation:
$$
H= ar{
u}_{l} M_{l l^{\prime}} 
u_{l} \quad l, l^{\prime}=e, \mu
$$
to determine the eigenstate masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ and the mixing angle.

reference_solution:
In the representation of $\nu_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $\nu_{\mu}$, the Hamiltonian is:
$$
H=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
M_{\mathrm{ee}} & M_{\mathrm{e} \mu} \\
M_{\mu e} & M_{\mu \mu}
\end{array}\right)
$$
Let $M_{e \mu}=M_{\mu e}$. The eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian can be found from:
$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(\begin{array}{cc}
M_{\mathrm{ee}}-m & M_{\mathrm{e} \mu} \\
M_{\mu e} & M_{\mu \mu}-m
\end{array}\right)=0 \\
m^{2}-\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}+M_{\mu \mu}\right) m+\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}} M_{\mu \mu}-M_{\mu e}^{2}\right)=0
\end{gathered}
$$

Solving this equation, we obtain:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& m_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}+M_{\mu \mu}\right)-\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}\right] \\
& m_{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}+M_{\mu \mu}\right)+\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}\right]
\end{aligned}
$$

Assuming that in the $\nu_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $\nu_{\mu}$ representation, $\nu_{2}$ is expressed as:
$$
\nu_{2}=\binom{a_{1}}{a_{2}}
$$
we have the equation:
$$
H \nu_{2}=m_{2} \nu_{2}
$$

which implies:
$$
\left(\begin{array}{cc}
M_{\mathrm{ee}} & M_{\mathrm{e} \mu} \\
M_{\mu \mathrm{e}} & M_{\mu \mu}
\end{array}\right)\binom{a_{1}}{a_{2}}=m_{2}\binom{a_{1}}{a_{2}}
$$

With the normalization condition $a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}=1$, we get:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& a_{1}=\frac{M_{\mu e}}{\sqrt{M_{\mu e}^{2}+\left(m_{2}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}\right)^{2}}} \\
& a_{2}=\frac{m_{2}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}}{\sqrt{M_{\mu e}^{2}+\left(m_{2}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}\right)^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the mixing relations:
$$
\begin{gathered}
\nu_{\mathrm{e}}=\nu_{1} \cos \theta+\nu_{2} \sin \theta \\
\nu_{\mu}=-\nu_{1} \sin \theta+\nu_{2} \cos \theta
\end{gathered}
$$

can be rewritten as:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \nu_{1}=\nu_{e} \cos \theta-\nu_{\mu} \sin \theta \\
& \nu_{2}=\nu_{e} \sin \theta+\nu_{\mu} \cos \theta
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $\nu_{2}=a_{1} \nu_{\mathrm{e}}+a_{2} \nu_{\mu}$, we find:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan \theta=\frac{a_{1}}{a_{2}} & =\frac{M_{\mu e}}{m_{2}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}}=\frac{2 M_{\mu e}}{M_{\mu \mu}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}+\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}} \\
\theta & =\arctan \frac{2 M_{\mu e}}{M_{\mu \mu}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}+\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{m_{1}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}+M_{\mu \mu}\right)-\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}\right], m_{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}+M_{\mu \mu}\right)+\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}\right], \theta = \arctan \frac{2 M_{\mu e}}{M_{\mu \mu}-M_{\mathrm{ee}}+\sqrt{\left(M_{\mathrm{ee}}-M_{\mu \mu}\right)^{2}+4 M_{\mu e}^{2}}}}

Solution (1)

$$  
\begin{gathered}  
L=-m c^{2} \sqrt{1-\frac{1}{c^{2}}\left(\dot{r}^{2}+r^{2} \dot{\varphi}^{2}\right)}+\frac{G M m}{r} \\  
\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{r}}=\frac{m \dot{r}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}, \quad \frac{\partial L}{\partial r}=\frac{m r \dot{\varphi}^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}-\frac{G M m}{r^{2}} \\  
\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{\varphi}}=\frac{m r^{2} \dot{\varphi}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}, \quad \frac{\partial L}{\partial \varphi}=0  
\end{gathered}  
$$

Where $v^{2}=\dot{r}^{2}+r^{2} \dot{\varphi}^{2}$,

\begin{gather*}  
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\left(\frac{m \dot{r}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}\right)-\left(\frac{m r \dot{\varphi}^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}-\frac{G M m}{r^{2}}\right)=0  \tag{1}\\  
\frac{m r^{2} \dot{\varphi}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}=J\ (\text{constant}) \tag{2}  
\end{gather*}

Substituting equation (2) into equation (1), we obtain

\begin{equation*}  
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\left(\frac{J \dot{r}}{r^{2} \dot{\varphi}}\right)-\frac{J \dot{\varphi}}{r}+\frac{G M m}{r^{2}}=0 \tag{3}  
\end{equation*}

Let $u=\frac{1}{r}, r=\frac{1}{u}, \dot{r}=-\frac{1}{u^{2}} \dot{u}, \frac{\dot{r}}{r^{2}}=-\dot{u}$,

$$  
\frac{\mathrm{d}^2 u}{\mathrm{~d} \varphi^2}=\frac{\mathrm{d} u}{\mathrm{~d} \varphi} \dot{\varphi}  
$$

Equation (3) can be transformed into

\begin{equation*}  
\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2} u}{\mathrm{~d} \varphi^{2}}+u=\frac{G M m}{J \dot{\varphi}} u^{2} \tag{4}  
\end{equation*}

Since $\frac{\partial L}{\partial t}=0$,

$$  
\begin{gathered}  
\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{r}} \dot{r}+\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{\varphi}} \dot{\varphi}-L=E\ (\text{constant}) \\  
\frac{m \dot{r}^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}-\frac{m r^{2} \dot{\varphi}^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}+m c^{2} \sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}-\frac{G M m}{r}=E  
\end{gathered}  
$$

We obtain

\begin{align*}  
& \frac{m c^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}-\frac{G M m}{r}=E \\  
& \frac{m c^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}}}=E+G M m u \tag{5}  
\end{align*}

From equation (2),

\begin{equation*}  
\dot{\varphi}=\frac{J}{m} \sqrt{1-\frac{v^{2}}{c^{2}}} u^{2} \tag{6}  
\end{equation*}

Using equations (5) and (6), equation (4) can be rewritten as

\begin{equation*}  
\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2} u}{\mathrm{~d} \varphi^{2}}+\left[1-\left(\frac{G M m}{J c}\right)^{2}\right] u=\frac{G M m E}{J^{2} c^{2}} \tag{7}  
\end{equation*}

Let

$$  
u^{\prime}=u-\frac{\frac{G M m E}{J^{2} c^{2}}}{1-\left(\frac{G M m}{J c}\right)^{2}}=u-\frac{G M m E}{(J c)^{2}-(G M m)^{2}}  
$$

Equation (7) becomes

$$  
\begin{gathered}  
\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2} u^{\prime}}{\mathrm{d} \varphi^{2}}+\left[1-\left(\frac{G M m}{J c}\right)^{2}\right] u^{\prime}=0 \\  
u^{\prime}=A \cos \left[\alpha\left(\varphi-\varphi_{0}\right)\right]  
\end{gathered}  
$$

Where

$$  
\alpha=\left[1-\left(\frac{G M m}{J c}\right)^{2}\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}  
$$

$A$ and $\varphi_{0}$ are constants.

$$  
u=\frac{G M m E}{(J c)^{2}-(G M m)^{2}}+A \cos \left[\alpha\left(\varphi-\varphi_{0}\right)\right]  
$$

$$  
\frac{1}{r}=\frac{1}{r_{0}}+A \cos \left[\alpha\left(\varphi-\varphi_{0}\right)\right]  
$$

where

$$  
\begin{gathered}  
r_{0}=\frac{(J c)^{2}-(G M m)^{2}}{G M m E} \\  
\frac{1}{r}=\frac{1}{r_{0}}\left\{1+\varepsilon \cos \left[\alpha\left(\varphi-\varphi_{0}\right)\right]\right\}  
\end{gathered}  
$$

where $\varepsilon=A r_{0}$.

Reference answer:
\boxed{\alpha=\left[1-\left(\frac{G M m}{J c}\right)^{2}\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}, r_{0}=\frac{(J c)^{2}-(G M m)^{2}}{G M m E}}

The Maxwell's equations in the context of the medium are

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{D}=0, \quad \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{B}=0 \\
& \nabla \times \boldsymbol{E}=-\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{B}}{\partial t}, \quad \nabla \times \boldsymbol{H}=\boldsymbol{J}+\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{D}}{\partial t}
\end{aligned}
$$

Note that the fields $\boldsymbol{E}$, $\boldsymbol{B}$ here are superpositions of the external fields and the fields generated by the time-varying magnetization intensity $\boldsymbol{M}(t)$, meaning they already include the fields produced by $\boldsymbol{M}(t)$.

Since it is an insulating medium and assuming the medium cannot carry charge, we have $\rho=0, \quad \boldsymbol{J}=0$. Given that $\varepsilon_{r}=1$, it follows that $\boldsymbol{D}=\varepsilon_{0} \boldsymbol{E}$. Therefore, the equations simplify to

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{E}=0, \quad \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{B}=0 \\
& \nabla \times \boldsymbol{E}=-\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{B}}{\partial t}, \quad \nabla \times \boldsymbol{H}=\varepsilon_{0} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{E}}{\partial t}, \quad \boldsymbol{B}=\mu \boldsymbol{H}
\end{aligned}
$$

From this, the wave equations are derived as

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \nabla^{2} \boldsymbol{E}-\mu \varepsilon_{0} \frac{\partial^{2} \boldsymbol{E}}{\partial t^{2}}=0 \\
& \nabla^{2} \boldsymbol{H}-\mu \varepsilon_{0} \frac{\partial^{2} \boldsymbol{H}}{\partial t^{2}}=0
\end{aligned}
$$

A plane wave solution to these equations is

$$
\boldsymbol{H}(t)=\boldsymbol{H}_{0} e^{i(kz-\omega t)}
$$

Substituting this into the wave equation, the dispersion relation can be obtained as

$$
k^{2}-\mu \varepsilon_{0} \omega^{2}=0, \quad k^{2}=\frac{\omega}{v_{p}}=\omega \sqrt{\mu(\omega) \varepsilon_{0}}=\frac{\omega}{c} \sqrt{\mu(\omega)}
$$

The magnetization intensity plane wave in the medium is

$$
\boldsymbol{M}(t)=\chi_{m}(\omega) \boldsymbol{H}(t)=\chi_{m} \boldsymbol{H}_{0} e^{i(kz-\omega t)}
$$

Thus, the dispersion relation for this plane wave remains

$$
k^{2}=\frac{\omega}{v_{p}}=\frac{\omega}{c} \sqrt{\mu(\omega)}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{k^{2} = \frac{\omega}{c} \sqrt{\mu(\omega)}}

