The system is composed of two localized subsystems, both of which are nearly independent. The number of particles in the two subsystems are \( N_{1} \) and \( N_{2} \), respectively; the energy and degeneracy of the single-particle energy levels are \( \varepsilon_{1 i}, g_{1 i} \) and \( \varepsilon_{2 j}, g_{2 j} \). The occupation numbers on these single-particle energy levels are \(\{a_{1 i}\}\) and \(\{a_{2 j}\}\), respectively. The distribution of the system in thermodynamic equilibrium is the most probable distribution, subject to the constraints of particle number and energy conservation:

\[
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i} a_{1 i} &= N_{1}  \tag{1}\\
\sum_{j} a_{2 j} &= N_{2}  \tag{2}\\
\sum_{i} a_{1 i} \varepsilon_{1 i} + \sum_{j} a_{2 j} \varepsilon_{2 j} &= E \tag{3}
\end{align*}
\]

(2) The distribution of the system in thermodynamic equilibrium: According to the principle of equal probability or the most probable principle, the equation determining the most probable distribution is:

\[
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial a_{1 i}}\left[\ln W - \alpha_{1} \sum_{i} a_{1 i} - \alpha_{2} \sum_{j} a_{2 j} - \beta\left(\sum_{i} a_{1 i} \varepsilon_{1 i} + \sum_{j} a_{2 j} \varepsilon_{2 j}\right)\right] &= 0  \tag{6}\\
\frac{\partial}{\partial a_{2 j}}\left[\ln W - \alpha_{1} \sum_{i} a_{1 i} - \alpha_{2} \sum_{j} a_{2 j} - \beta\left(\sum_{i} a_{1 i} \varepsilon_{1 i} + \sum_{j} a_{2 j} \varepsilon_{2 j}\right)\right] &= 0 \tag{7}
\end{align*}
\]

In the expressions, \(\alpha_{1}, \alpha_{2}, \beta\) are the three Lagrange multipliers. From equations (6) and (7), the distribution in thermodynamic equilibrium can be obtained as:

\[
\begin{align*}
\overline{a_{1 i}} &= g_{1 i} \exp \left[-\left(\alpha_{1} + \beta \varepsilon_{1 i}\right)\right] = \frac{N_{1}}{z_{1}} g_{1 i} \exp \left(-\beta \varepsilon_{1 i}\right)  \tag{8}\\
\overline{a_{2 j}} &= g_{2 j} \exp \left[-\left(\alpha_{2} + \beta \varepsilon_{2 j}\right)\right] = \frac{N_{2}}{z_{2}} g_{2 j} \exp \left(-\beta \varepsilon_{2 j}\right) \tag{9}
\end{align*}
\]

Here, \(z_{1}\) and \(z_{2}\) are the single-particle partition functions for the two subsystems, respectively:

