\[
\sigma_{\mathrm D}=e n_{\mathrm D}\mu_{\mathrm D},
\qquad
\sigma_{\mathrm n}=e n\mu_{\mathrm n}.
\]

For a magnetic field \(\mathbf B=B\hat{\mathbf z}\), the Hall angle of each type of electron satisfies

\[
\tan\theta_i=-\mu_iB.
\]

Thus, defining the positive transverse conductivity magnitude, the total conductivity components are

\[
\sigma_{xx}
=e\left[
\frac{n_{\mathrm D}\mu_{\mathrm D}}{1+\mu_{\mathrm D}^{2}B^{2}}
+
\frac{n\mu_{\mathrm n}}{1+\mu_{\mathrm n}^{2}B^{2}}
\right],
\]

\[
\sigma_H
=eB\left[
\frac{n_{\mathrm D}\mu_{\mathrm D}^{2}}{1+\mu_{\mathrm D}^{2}B^{2}}
+
\frac{n\mu_{\mathrm n}^{2}}{1+\mu_{\mathrm n}^{2}B^{2}}
\right].
\]

The conductivity tensor and its inverse are

\[
\boldsymbol{\sigma}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_{xx}&-\sigma_H\\
\sigma_H&\sigma_{xx}
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\boldsymbol{\rho}=\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-1}.
\]

Under the Hall measurement condition \(j_y=0\),

\[
R_H=\frac{E_y}{j_xB}
=-\frac{\sigma_H}{B\left(\sigma_{xx}^{2}+\sigma_H^{2}\right)}.
